"Counterflow Heat Exchanger — Oil Cooled with Water

A counterflow heat exchanger with a heat transfer area of 12.5 m2 is used
to cool oil (c = 2100 J/(kg.K)) at 100 C with water at 1 bar and 20 C.
Oil and water flow rates are 2 and 0.48 kg/s respectively.
Global heat transfer coefficient: U = 400 W/(m2.K).
Determine the outlet temperatures of both fluids and the transferred power.
(Pressure drops in the heat exchanger are neglected.)
"
A=12.5 "[m^2]"
c_oil=2100 "[J/g.K]"
T_oil_su=100 "[°C]"
P_w_su=1E5 "[Pa]"
T_w_su=20
M_dot_oil=2 "[kg/s]"
M_dot_w=0.48 "[kg/s]"
U=400 "[W/m^2.K]"

c_w=CP(Water,T=T_w_su,P=P_w_su)

"capacity flow rates:"
C_dot_oil=M_dot_oil*c_oil
C_dot_w=M_dot_w*c_w
C_dot_min=min(C_dot_oil,C_dot_w)
C_dot_max=max(C_dot_oil,C_dot_w)
omega=C_dot_min/C_dot_max

"NTU"
AU=A*U
NTU=AU/C_dot_min

"heat exchanger effectiveness"
epsilon=(1-exp(-NTU*(1-omega)))/(1-omega*exp(-NTU*(1-omega)))

"energy balance"
Q_dot=epsilon*C_dot_min*(T_oil_su-T_w_su) "[W]"
Q_dot=C_dot_w*(T_w_ex-T_w_su)
Q_dot=C_dot_oil*(T_oil_su-T_oil_ex)